
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
</code>

In the second block of code, the comment start with "@@@" instructs to verify that, if the return value is non-negative, x is still in the old array list. Therefore, the assertion checks that the new return value is not negative (i.e., x is not in the list) and whether the x is still contained in the old array list.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX == true);
</code>
After reading through given code, it seems that the "If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list" is the statement made in the comment "@@@" in the code which is checking if x is in old array list (Old_arrListContainsX). 

However, it's necessary to note that the actual condition depends on the actual implementation of the "LastIndexOf" method. It might be different than the above mentioned. We can simply use the condition as per the comment, assuming that "x" is in the old array list when we call "LastIndexOf" for "x".--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX == true);
</code>

<code>
